{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Naphtali is a hind let loose</b>. It is the custom among the rulers of lands to send harts and hinds to one another. And so the meaning is: the hinds born in the land of the king of the north are raised in the palaces of the king of the south, and they tie a written message to her horns and she runs swiftly and returns to her dwelling, and through them they receive tidings. And this is the sense of \"words of fair\" — that is, a hind let loose brings glad tidings. And this matter is well known, and it is mentioned in the Yerushalmi in tractate Shevi'it (9:2). They said: if they send them forth — they return. And if you wish to test it, they would take deer and send them to a distant land; in the end they return. So one did: he took deer and plated their horns with silver, and sent them to Africa, and at the end of thirteen years they returned to their place — for they had released them after thirteen years and immediately they returned. And the parable is that Naphtali is sated with favor and full of every good, and from him will come glad tidings to all Israel, for his land produced fruit in abundance, as our Rabbis mentioned concerning the fruits of Ginossar (Tanhuma Vayehi 13):"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {0 "The Yerushalmi passage is rendered in its original Aramaic idiom into English; the paraphrased summary that follows (\"for they had released them...\") is Ramban's own Hebrew gloss on the Aramaic anecdote."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:47:10.080516686Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}